<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Garifuna</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Garinagu, Black Caribs</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Garifuna are a people of mixed African and Indigenous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> descent who formed on Saint Vincent from the 17th century, known to the British as Black Caribs. After their defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-carib-war">Second Carib War</a> most were deported in 1797 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roatan">Roatán</a>, and they spread along the Caribbean coast of Honduras, Belize, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> and Nicaragua. Their language, music and dance are recognised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> as Intangible Cultural Heritage.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/garifuna.jpg" alt="Garifuna" width="320" height="345" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Agostino Brunias (ca. 1730 – 2 April 1796) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_Caribs-family-St.-Vincent_by_Augustino_Brunias.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Garifuna are an Afro-Indigenous people of mixed free African and Amerindian ancestry who originated on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> island of Saint Vincent, which they call Yurumein, and traditionally speak Garifuna, an Arawakan language. They descend from Indigenous Arawak and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinago">Kalinago</a> (Island Carib) people and Afro-Caribbean people. European writers called them Black Caribs, and modern historians have said that British colonial use of the name framed most of the island's Indigenous population as interlopers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> without claims to land.</p><p>How the African part of the population arrived is debated. In 1795 the British governor William Young wrote that Africans from two Spanish slave ships wrecked near Saint Vincent in 1635, largely Ibibio, had lived independently and integrated with the Indigenous people. Several modern researchers hold that most came from other islands, such as Barbados, Saint Lucia and Grenada, as people escaping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, and were taken in by the Caribs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1763 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1763">Treaty of Paris</a> gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> control. In the First Carib War, which began in 1769, the Black Carib chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-chatoyer">Joseph Chatoyer</a> led a successful defence of the windward side of the island, and a peace agreement of 1773 set boundaries between British and Carib areas. In 1795 the Caribs rebelled again, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-carib-war">Second Carib War</a>, and gained control of most of the island; a major expedition under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ralph-abercromby">Ralph Abercromby</a> defeated them in 1796.</p><p>The British authorities then decided to deport the Caribs, to avoid further slave revolts. In 1797 those with African features, considered the cause of the revolt, were chosen, taken first to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> and then to the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roatan">Roatán</a> in Honduras, while Black Caribs with more Amerindian traits were allowed to remain. More than 5,000 were deported, but only about 2,500 had survived when they landed on Roatán on 12 April 1797. They expanded along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>, to Belize and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> in the north and Nicaragua in the south, and became known as Garifuna.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Almost all Garinagu are bilingual or multilingual and generally speak the official languages of their countries as a first language, while many speak Garifuna mainly as a cultural language. In 2001 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> proclaimed the language, dance and music of the Garifuna a Masterpiece of the Oral and Intangible Heritage of Humanity in Nicaragua, Honduras and Belize. Most Garinagu are Catholic, and a traditional religion honours the ancestors and Bungiu, or Sunti Gabafu, with a shaman called a buyei at its head.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Garifuna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On Saint Vincent, Kalinago and free West Africans formed the <b>Garifuna</b>; after the Second Carib War 4,338 Garifuna were deported to Roatan.</blockquote><a href="/culture/kalinago">Kalinago · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Its population mixes Kriol, Mestizo, Maya, <b>Garifuna</b> and Mennonite communities.</blockquote><a href="/state/belize">Belize</a></li><li><blockquote>The population mixed Creoles, <b>Garifuna</b>, Maya and Mestizo refugees from the Caste War.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-honduras">British Honduras</a></li><li><blockquote>Saint Vincent was largely held by the <b>Garifuna</b> and Kalinago until the Treaty of Paris of 1763 granted it to Britain.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-saint-vincent">Colony of Saint Vincent</a></li><li><blockquote>From the 1830s English-speaking settlers, many from the Cayman Islands and other British Caribbean islands, settled on Roatán, Utila and Guanaja, where <b>Garifuna</b> communities already lived.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-the-bay-islands">Colony of the Bay Islands</a></li><li><blockquote>By around 1700 these <b>Black Caribs</b>, the Garinagu, formed a distinct people controlling much of the island's windward side, separate from the Yellow Caribs.</blockquote><a href="/state/garifuna-of-saint-vincent">Garifuna of Saint Vincent</a></li></ul></section>
